Title: The sitter uninstalls itself: update_url advertises zero versions
Created: 2026-09-06
Author: sim

--- log ---
2026-09-06T21:42Z sim created
2026-09-06T21:44Z claude note Filed from a live reproduction on doudou, the first this defect has had. Ticket 0688 closed with the cause explicitly OPEN and "needs live reproduction, which no code here can perform"; this is that reproduction, with a mechanism that fits every observation 0688 recorded and was not among the three it considered.

2026-09-06T22:34Z claude note THE update_url MECHANISM IN THIS TICKET'S TITLE IS NOT SUPPORTED BY THE EVENING'S EVIDENCE, and the title is left standing only so the id stays greppable. Two things refuted it. First, the fix built on it did not work: 0.2.15 shipped with update.json advertising a compatible version and was disabled at +20 s and deleted at +40 s, exactly as 0.2.13 had been with the empty list. Second, the timing never fit — `extensions.update.interval` is 86400, a daily check, against a disable that arrived 20 seconds after every install. That was visible before the fix was written and was not weighed heavily enough.
2026-09-06T22:34Z claude note What the watcher actually recorded (scratchpad/sitter-watch.log, six cycles): every disable-then-delete happened inside ONE Zotero process, pid 635164, up since 20:56, into which the add-on had already been installed and force-removed at least once. The first install into a FRESH process — pid 1057602, started 00:30:04 — went active at 00:30:43 and stayed. The author's own reading of the same window agrees: with the stale process he saw a frozen toolbar button showing message ids and not pulsing, which is an orphaned button from a dead instance rather than anything the running build rendered; after the restart the strings were English immediately.
2026-09-06T22:34Z claude note So the candidate to test is process state, not the update manifest: a Zotero session that has already had this add-on installed and removed under it appears unable to keep it. That is a sharper claim than the one this ticket opened with, it explains the author's original report (the add-on disappearing "on its own" during long sessions), and it is falsifiable — install into a fresh process and leave it, then install into a session that has already removed it once, and compare. Neither arm was run deliberately tonight; both happened by accident, which is why this is a candidate and not a finding.
2026-09-06T22:34Z claude note Also settled tonight, with a control, and independent of all the above: update_url is REQUIRED. A build identical but for its removal (0.2.14) is refused at install with "peut-etre incompatible avec cette version de Zotero", which corrects ticket 0688's docs-based reading that only applications.zotero must be present. The update.json entry added on this ticket stays for that reason, and the guard keeping it in step with manifest.json stays with it.
2026-09-06T22:53Z claude note REFINED, and this is the sharpest evidence the ticket has. 0.3.1 was installed at 00:50:48 into pid 1057602 -- the same process that had held 0.3.0 active for forty minutes -- and was gone, record and file, at 00:51:08. Twenty seconds, in the process the previous note called a survivor. So "a fresh process survives" is wrong as stated. What fits every cycle recorded tonight is narrower: the FIRST install into a Zotero process survives, and a REPLACEMENT into the same process does not. pid 635164 took six installs and lost all six, every one of them a second-or-later install in that process; pid 1057602 kept its first (0.3.0, forty minutes and counting when it was replaced) and lost its second (0.3.1, twenty seconds). It also explains the author's original report without needing update_url at all: he updates builds inside a long-running Zotero, which is exactly the replacement case. The prediction is falsifiable and cheap -- quit Zotero, start it, install as the FIRST install, and it should stick; then install anything over it in the same session and it should die. Neither arm has been run deliberately yet.
2026-09-08T04:50Z claude note Attempted the deliberate test the previous note called for, with the author's permission to use his live running Zotero on doudou. It did not run: bench/sdt_sitter_install.py's persistent-install method -- copy a bare XPI to <profile>/extensions/<id>.xpi -- did NOT register in extensions.json, not even after quitting Zotero and relaunching it fresh (pid 3389020), polled every 10s for 260s with zero transitions. This is a THIRD, previously unrecorded failure mode, and it is upstream of everything else this ticket has measured: every prior data point in this log (0.2.13 through 0.3.1, all of it) was a REPLACEMENT of an add-on ID Zotero had already learned via the GUI's "Install Add-on From File" flow (that is how fulltext-control@search-works-for-zotero.invalid -- the one other add-on in this profile -- got its entry: installTelemetryInfo.method is literally "install-from-file"). Nobody has, on this host, gotten the FIRST-ever registration of this add-on's id to happen via a bare-file drop; ticket 0688's own bench script docstring claims it "survives a restart," and that claim was never re-verified against this Zotero build (10.0.1_20260824184709) and does not hold today. Cause not established -- candidates include a signing/scope restriction Zotero enforces on file-scope installs that GUI install-from-file does not hit, or simply a required rescan trigger neither restart nor 260s of wall time provides. GUI automation to work around it (xdotool) is not viable in this session: xdotool sees no Zotero window at all (only GNOME Shell/mutter chrome), consistent with Zotero running as a native Wayland client this X11 tool cannot enumerate. Cleaned up: removed the dropped XPI, profile now back to its pre-experiment state (only fulltext-control installed). This blocks Action 1 exactly as it now stands (need a way to get a build installed at all before disable/enable or first/replacement can be tested) and is the reason ticket 0730's Action 1 also stayed unanswered today.
2026-09-08T07:31Z claude note THREE ARMS RUN DELIBERATELY ON DOUDOU THIS MORNING, THE FIRST TIME ANY OF THEM HAS BEEN. Two hypotheses were tested and BOTH ARE REFUTED. The ticket is further from a cause than it was last night, and that is a result rather than a setback: the two readings that felt strongest were the ones nobody had tried to falsify. Timings are anchored on Zotero's own `updateDate` in extensions.json, not on a stopwatch; state was sampled every 2 s straight out of extensions.json; liveness was judged on the cache file's compact write, which `bootstrap.js:1209` makes unconditional on the first save of every activation (`if (!compact && !changes.length) return` does not fire while `compact` is true) and `:1367` triggers at the end of every census.
2026-09-08T07:31Z claude note ARM 1 — FIRST INSTALL, held. 0.3.3 installed at 08:16:38 into pid 3389020, a process that had never held the add-on, through Tools -> Add-ons -> Install Add-on From File. Present and active 45 min later with zero transitions in the watcher log. Stated in advance and repeated here because it is what makes arm 2 meaningful: A QUIET ARM 1 PROVES NOTHING BY ITSELF. "First installs survive" and "the phenomenon stopped happening" emit the identical trace, which is an empty log.
2026-09-08T07:31Z claude note ARM 2 — REPLACEMENT, held; the 22:53 hypothesis is REFUTED. 0.3.4 installed at 09:07:13 OVER the live 0.3.3, same process, same menu, no removal first. Its bootstrap.js and scheduler.js are BYTE-IDENTICAL to the running build (3f518e98..., cfbe49ba...); the manifest version is the only difference, one character, so the arm isolates the act of replacing and nothing else. Prediction from last night's seven deaths: disable at ~+20 s, delete at ~+40 s. Observed: alive and active at 15 minutes, 40x the predicted delay, and demonstrably WORKING rather than merely registered — cache rewritten at 09:10:48, 3 min 35 s after install. pid 3389020 is the matched analogue of last night's pid 1057602, which kept its first install and lost its second in 20 s. So "the FIRST install into a process survives and a REPLACEMENT does not" is false as stated.
2026-09-08T07:31Z claude note ARM 3 — THE PRE-FIX PAYLOAD, held; MY OWN CANDIDATE IS REFUTED, and it is recorded because a discarded hypothesis costs the next reader nothing to skip and a great deal to re-derive. Having refuted arm 2 I proposed that this morning's `const`->`var` conversion (ticket 0730, PR #444, merged 07:01) was what stopped the deaths: if Zotero re-ran bootstrap.js into an already-used scope, every replacement before the fix threw `SyntaxError: Identifier 'closeJournalled' has already been declared` at parse time, startup() never ran, and the host cleaned up a broken add-on. Built 0.3.5 carrying the PRE-fix bootstrap.js (3ac5bf73..., its 12 top-level const/let intact) with scheduler.js identical to the running build — one variable. Positive control run before shipping it, in both directions: the old copy reddens with the exact SyntaxError, the running copy double-loads clean. Installed over 0.3.4 at 09:24:26. It survives, and it is fully alive: it ASKED THE LAUNCH QUESTION (which is called inside initialize(), so a parse failure could not have produced it), its button pulses (armed at :1394, AFTER the confirm at :1388), and its census completed with a cache write at 09:30:42.
2026-09-08T07:31Z claude note WHAT ARM 3 SETTLES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT — 0730's open question, one notch short. Ticket 0730 said its whole question was "whether Zotero ever re-runs bootstrap.js into a scope it has already used", and closed unable to answer it. Arm 3 answers it FOR THE UPGRADE PATH: no. A replaced add-on gets a fresh scope, the pre-fix file evaluates cleanly, and the double-load defect was latent and harmless exactly as 0730 guessed it might be. The `var` conversion stands as defence in depth, not as a cure. BUT 0730 asked specifically about DISABLE/RE-ENABLE — "turn the add-on off, then on again", the recovery its own UI recommends — and that is a different Gecko path which nothing here touched. It now costs one toggle to settle, and it is the cheapest experiment left in this ticket.
2026-09-08T07:31Z claude note WHAT REMAINS, so nobody re-runs a refuted arm. Something distinguishes 2026-09-06 from 2026-09-08 and it is not the replacement gesture and not the const/var payload. Three candidates survive, none tested: (a) last night's builds differed substantially from each other, so a defect peculiar to those payloads is not excluded; (b) the install GESTURE of last night cannot be reconstructed — this morning established separately that a bare XPI dropped into <profile>/extensions/ never registers at all, even across a restart, so whatever registered last night was not that, but which menu path it was is not recorded anywhere; (c) the phenomenon is intermittent, which would produce exactly today's picture and which no short experiment can exclude. Do not re-run arms 2 or 3 without a new reason: they were run deliberately, with controls, and they answered.
2026-09-08T07:42Z claude note ARM 3 CLOSED FORMALLY at 15 minutes, no transition, and the live journals of the same session are frozen as evidence in bench/results/sdt-sitter-2026-09-08/ — the in-session ring is volatile by design, so unwritten they would simply have ceased to exist. Two captures, and the second is a finding this ticket did not go looking for: once the load fell and the gate opened, the 39 pending documents were all admitted and ALL 39 FAILED, each in 50-90 ms, every one under the bare class `Error`. That is far too fast to be extraction losing, and 39 identical outcomes across sizes from 132 kB to 660 kB argue for something systemic upstream rather than for hard documents. It bears directly on 0728 and 0606 and is recorded there rather than chased here.
2026-09-08T07:42Z claude note SIDE OBSERVATION, correlation only, cause not established. The 0.3.5's census took 6 min 16 s (install 09:24:26 -> cache 09:30:42) against the 0.3.4's 3 min 35 s. Both activations re-MD5 the whole library, because `createSDTSourceHashes`'s memo is built in initialize() and is therefore empty at every activation, and because in inspect() the hash is computed BEFORE identity, which is the key the persistent cache is queried under — so the on-disk cache structurally cannot short-circuit it. That is the pre-release safety finding of 2026-09-08 showing up as wall-clock time on a live library; it is not evidence about this ticket's disappearance.
2026-09-08T10:03Z claude note ARM 4 — DISABLE-THEN-RE-ENABLE, the transition this ticket's "What remains" note called the cheapest experiment left, and the first test of it on a SECOND machine (padme, not doudou). First-ever install of this add-on id into a fresh, never-before-used Zotero profile/process, from a 0.3.3 package built off current main at `4ca7662b` (bootstrap.js 3f518e9861af6a78e87de9317cfa502b469246bc99b2fadcd6f2307fe3bf085f, scheduler.js cfbe49bac75be426e38e55f3ef4cd7aea7d2394f15875f01d25d4917e3f5c6d0 — byte-identical to the running build arms 2 and 3 used on doudou). Author installed via Tools -> Add-ons -> Install Add-on From File; the census was still MID-RUN, indexing some documents, when the author disabled the add-on, then re-enabled it in the same running session with no Zotero restart in between. Reported conversationally rather than instrumented, so exact timestamps and elapsed time are NOT captured here — this is a coarser data point than arms 1-3, not a replacement for them. Result: the add-on SURVIVED — it stayed listed and came back active, which is what this ticket tracks. It did NOT resume the interrupted census: re-enable re-asked the launch confirmation dialog and restarted the census phase from the top, and the author flagged that the in-progress indexing from before the disable may not have been cached. Whether that non-resume is a defect or by design is a separate question from this ticket's disappearance mechanism — DECISIONS.md's 2026-09-05 ratification of the disposable cache explicitly withholds any durable active-job or failure ledger, so a restarted census on re-enable may be the intended behavior rather than a bug; if the author wants that examined, it belongs against 0685/0740 (the cache design), not here. For THIS ticket: every deliberately-tested transition across two machines and two builds (first install, replacement, pre-fix payload, and now disable/re-enable, this one mid-census) has now survived; the only occurrences on record remain the two organic ones from 2026-09-06 on doudou, neither reproduced since. This shifts weight in the "What remains" list toward (c) intermittency or (a) build-specific, away from any transition being the trigger by itself.

2026-09-08T10:10Z claude note closing per author's instruction, on the record so the reason is not lost: four deliberate arms across two machines and two builds (first install, replacement, pre-fix payload, disable/re-enable) all SURVIVED — none reproduces the disappearance. Recorded here so the closure reads accurately: this is NOT a "not reproducible" verdict on the underlying defect, since the defect itself is not in question — it occurred twice on 2026-09-06 with hard evidence (extensions.json mutation, the XPI actually deleted from disk, mid-session). What is unreproduced is the TRIGGER. Closing as parked/downgraded rather than disproven: the remaining live candidate is intermittency (see "What remains" above), which no bounded experiment excludes. Reopen on any future recurrence rather than filing a fresh ticket — the accumulated arms are the reason this one should keep the history.

