2026-09-11T16:15Z claude note GAP found reading arm 5 back against this ticket's own updated Test section (the sibling edit that landed on main mid-run): the positive control it requires -- provoke a transition deliberately and watch the watcher emit a line -- was never satisfied for the DISABLED state. run-watch.txt has zero active=False lines across 17 deliberate disable() calls, because run_cycle disables then immediately re-enables with no pause, and extensions.json's active field write is debounced (confirmed separately in 0766: isActive:true live immediately after enable(), on-disk active field still false moments later). Does not weaken the disappearance claim itself -- present/absent and xpi-on-disk are not debounced, and the absent->present direction of that exact transition was watched working at cycle 1 -- but it does mean this arm never positive-controlled the disabled-state read specifically. Added to verification/SDT-SITTER-DISAPPEARANCE-0727.md's Arm 5 section; whoever extends this arm should add a deliberate pause between disable and enable as its own positive control.
2026-09-12T07:26Z claude note INSTRUMENTATION ROUND, six gaps closed. The author asked whether this ticket is instrumented if the phenomenon recurs; the honest answer was half, and the half that was missing is the half that matters. What was already good and nobody had noticed: shutdown(data, reason) reads GECKO'S OWN reason for the teardown (SHUTDOWN_REASONS maps ADDON_DISABLE/ADDON_UNINSTALL/APP_SHUTDOWN to a name), that record is the last thing written to the ring, and the ring is then parked at Zotero.SDTPackSitterJournal -- so the answer to "which event was it", the question this ticket has been unable to answer since 2026-09-06, already exists in the live process. What was missing: (1) 0764 Action 5's recovery line said "restart Zotero" FIRST, and a restart destroys that parked ring -- reordered, read before restarting, and the Run JavaScript incantation is now written out in both the ticket and the report; (2) nothing kept a copy that outlives the process -- the death certificate now writes the redacted ring plus the named reason to sdt-sitter-last-shutdown.json in the data directory, on the disable and uninstall reasons ONLY (an ordinary quit would bury the two events that matter under a year of them), and gated on the existing debug pref: on in a bench profile, off in a released build, per the author's ruling of 2026-09-12. That gate is what keeps it inside SPEC.md's "no private durable ledger": it is a certificate of death, one file, overwritten, written only when the plugin is being taken away, carrying composeSDTJournalReport's clipboard-boundary redaction (opaque keys, no titles, no rootURI). Written synchronously through Zotero.File.putContents because the sandbox does not outlive shutdown on the uninstall path and an async write is a certificate that may never land; (3) the watcher existed only INSIDE bench/sitter_volume_experiment.py, so nothing could watch the author's own profile, which is where both organic occurrences happened -- extracted to bench/sitter_watch.py with a CLI, and the driver now imports it rather than keeping a second copy; (4) the watcher kept only its own alert line, which is what the two organic occurrences already produced -- it now preserves extensions.json before the host rewrites it, the parked ring over RDP when a port is given, and the certificate, each reported as taken or as absent because an absent one is a finding too; (5) the host's own reasoning was never captured -- extensions.logging.enabled, plus Zotero's debug.log/debug.store, are now seeded into every throwaway profile the rig builds, and the report says to set them by hand on a live one; (6) the disabled-state read had no positive control (the 16:15Z entry's gap) -- run_cycle now holds the disabled state for --disable-hold-seconds, default 6 s, so every cycle must leave an active=False line and a run without one proves nothing about disabled states. Evidence: four scenarios in tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs and five mutants M40-M44 (an ordinary quit certifies, the pref stops gating, only disable is certified, the envelope invents its own reason, the ring goes unredacted), 44/44 caught; seven arms in tests/test_sitter_watch.py, each silence arm paired with the arm that makes it mean something. 0.3.44. NOTHING HERE IS A CAUSE: the ticket is exactly as far from a mechanism as it was this morning. What changed is that the next occurrence leaves evidence instead of a gap.
2026-09-12T08:32Z claude note REVIEW ROUND on the instrumentation above, read against this repo's own automatic-monitoring experience rather than as fresh code. Seven findings, all fixed, and TWO OF THEM WERE LESSONS THIS TICKET HAD ALREADY PAID FOR. (1) The watcher collapsed host_addon_record's three-valued read into one boolean before deciding whether to fire, so an unreadable extensions.json -- a partial read during one of the restarts the author performs himself, a case arm 5 never exercised because its one process never restarted -- would have fired the alert and spent the evidence taking on a non-event. That is the same "all-clear indistinguishable from could-not-look" trap that let the 16:15Z gap through. Fixed: an unreadable reading is logged, does not fire, and does NOT update _was_present, so present -> unreadable -> absent still fires on the third reading; arm added, with the fire as its own positive control. (2) The one RDP read that cannot be repeated -- the parked ring at a real alert -- was a bare single-shot connect, while every routine call in the volume driver goes through connect_resilient precisely because a bare connect was found unreliable against this host in 0766. connect_resilient moved into bench/sitter_watch.py (one implementation, the driver imports it) and the ring read now uses it; two arms, with connect and sleep injectable. (3) The missing-certificate message named two causes and omitted the third the plugin itself guards for -- a failed write from an unwritable or full data directory, which this repo has ticket history for -- so on a real occurrence the log would have pointed the investigator at the wrong two hypotheses. (4) The driver still had no SIGTERM handler: the same round that fixed six of arm 5's gaps left the one that orphaned a headless Zotero when the author cut it short. Handled now, through the same teardown as a clean end. (5) "putContents writes before returning" was asserted in a comment and checked nowhere -- a weaker standard than 0766 held its own host claims to. The certificate now READS ITSELF BACK and journals certificate-failed if it did not land, which also covers a file system that accepts a write and keeps nothing. (6) composeSDTJournalReport answers localized PROSE on its error path, and that was being filed under `report`: a certificate whose body does not parse, at exactly the shutdown that matters, with nothing saying so. It goes under `reportUnreadable` now. (7) The .log/.gitignore trap that already cost a rename on 2026-09-11 was documented but not prevented; both CLIs refuse a .log target at the door. Evidence: 46/46 bootstrap mutants (M45 the write believed unread, M46 prose filed as a report; M43 and M44 re-anchored on the rewritten function), 11 arms in tests/test_sitter_watch.py, node suites green. 0.3.45. Still no cause: this round, like the one before it, only buys evidence for the next occurrence.
2026-09-12T09:42Z claude note THE EXPERIMENT NOW EXPLORES INSTEAD OF WAITING. Asked whether it fuzzes at all, the answer was no, and the diagnosis was worse than the question: every cycle built a byte-identical payload (version string apart, the isolation discipline arm 2 established, which was right for arm 2 and wrong carried into a volume run), at two fixed cadences, in one process never restarted, over an EMPTY profile -- created by mkdir plus prefs.js, no items, no attachments. So the sitter under test was IDLE throughout, and arm 5's negative result does not say what its own log implies: not "17 rapid replacements do not kill the sitter" but "17 rapid replacements do not kill an idle sitter over an empty library in a process that is never restarted". Both organic occurrences were in the author's real library with extraction under way -- native worker handles held, cache being written, disk touched. Three axes opened, in the order they matter. (1) THE LIBRARY: --menagerie imports the Multilingual Menagerie (0721) into the throwaway profile before the first install, via Zotero.Translate.Import with linkFiles:true -- the same sequence bench/zotero-fulltext-plugin's /import endpoint runs, lifted rather than reached through, because installing a second add-on into the profile under test is installing into the experiment. --warmup-seconds then lets it census and start extracting before anything is pulled out from under it. The RIS names 114 attachments by relative path and their bytes come from the recipe fetch cache, which is on doudou, so the package must be assembled there with make menagerie-package; a package whose files are not on disk is REFUSED, since records without attachments leave the sitter as idle as an empty library. Running with no library at all now requires --allow-empty-library and says so in the log. (2) RESTARTS: --restart-every N stops Zotero and brings it back, reattaching. The one axis both occurrences had -- long sessions the author restarts himself -- and that no arm has ever touched. The watcher stays ARMED across it: an add-on gone once Zotero is back is the phenomenon in the shape originally reported, and the unreadable window a restart opens is exactly what this morning's poll_once fix refuses to read as an absence. (3) RANDOMISATION: --seed draws each cycle's action from a weighted table (replace, replace-disable-enable, replace-disable-immediately, uninstall-then-install, double-install) and its interval log-uniformly from 2 s to 10 min -- log-uniform because a uniform draw over that range spends nine tenths of its cycles above a minute and never revisits the rapid succession the 2026-09-08 recurrence had. The fixed burst/spaced script is KEPT and is what runs without a seed, as the control. The seed and the ENTIRE PLAN are written to the log before the first cycle: a run that finally reproduces has to be replayable from its log alone, or the reproduction is a story rather than a finding. One design point worth the code: uninstall-then-install produces the watcher's own signature deliberately, so it runs inside a new Watcher.expect_absence window -- otherwise the instrument would manufacture the defect it is watching for, and a false reproduction is worse than none because it would be investigated and the investigation would end in the driver's own log. Twelve arms now in tests/test_sitter_watch.py (the expectation window, and that it does not outlive the gesture), eight in tests/test_sitter_volume_plan.py (the deterministic arm is unchanged; a seed replays exactly and a different seed does not; the draw reaches every action and both ends of the clock; the median interval proves the draw is log-uniform and not uniform). No plugin change, so no version bump. Still no cause -- but the next run will at last be looking in the state the defect was seen in.
2026-09-12T10:06Z claude note REVIEW ROUND on the widening, and it found the widening standing on air. Seven findings, all fixed, two of them fatal to the point of the exercise. THE FIRST IS 0778's, arrived independently from the lifecycle audit and confirmed here: --headless gives initialize() no main window, so alive never becomes true -- no census, no cache write, no timers. Giving the rig a library does nothing for a plugin that never runs, and arms 1-4 had a liveness reading (the cache file's compact write) that arm 5 dropped and replaced with nothing. The rig now READS liveness after the warmup (the host handle, the phase, scanned, and whether the cache file exists) and REFUSES to cycle unless the sitter armed, naming 0778 and Xvfb in the refusal; --allow-dead-sitter says you mean to test Zotero's bookkeeping alone, and logs that the run says nothing about a running sitter. THE SECOND: the launch question is a synchronous native confirmEx modal, asked once and persisted forever, and nothing pre-answered it -- headless there is nobody to answer, the RDP client's own docstring records an unanswered native prompt hanging forever, and a defaulted answer would have disarmed the sitter for the whole run. extensions.sdt-pack-sitter.enabled is now seeded beside the debug pref. Also fixed: the import counted a RESOLVED PATH as a file on disk (getFilePathAsync, where the /import endpoint it copies uses fileExists -- the sitter's own code proves the difference, since after getFilePathAsync it still stats and catches NotFoundError), so a package with no bytes would have passed the gate that exists to refuse exactly that; the import ran before Zotero.initializationPromise, on a profile whose schema has never been created, which is where that race would fire; the watcher looked for the death certificate beside prefs.js, but the data directory is NOT the profile directory -- it is read over RDP now and the watcher is pointed at it, or every real occurrence would have reported the certificate absent; a failed relaunch raised past the per-cycle RDP recovery into the FATAL handler and cost the whole multi-hour budget, now retried once; --max-minutes counted the import and the warmup against the cycling budget; double-install was renamed replace-twice because the install eval awaits onInstallEnded, so the two are sequential and the old name promised a race that is not tested. And one design correction with teeth: expect_absence swallowed ANY absence in its window, so a genuine occurrence landing inside one of the six-second uninstall windows -- one cycle in ten, over hours -- would have been filed as expected and lost. The window now takes the evidence anyway, and when it closes it checks the record came back: a gesture that always ends with a reinstall leaving the add-on still gone is not the gesture, and fires. 14 watcher arms, 9 plan arms, 1347 pytest, 46/46 mutants. Cross-reference added: this is NOT ticket 0700, which wants the LIVE Menagerie group and its member key; neither supersedes the other.
2026-09-12T10:08Z claude note DATA-SAFETY DEFECT found while writing the run instructions for padme, and it predates every arm. bench/sitter_volume_experiment.py's docstring has said since arm 5 that it runs "against a THROWAWAY profile, never the author's real library". The PROFILE was throwaway; the DATA DIRECTORY never was. Nothing seeded extensions.zotero.dataDir, so a fresh profile opens Zotero's DEFAULT data directory -- which on both of the author's machines is his real library (verification/VERIFY-FULLTEXT-SQLITE.md records /home/haduong/data/Zotero). Arm 5 survived it only because it never imported anything and the sitter never armed; with the Menagerie import added this would have imported 103 records into the author's own library and let the sitter extract over it. The pref is now pinned into the seeded prefs.js, --data-dir names it, it defaults to <profile>-data beside the throwaway profile, and a directory already holding a zotero.sqlite is REFUSED as somebody's library. The watcher's certificate lookup follows the same directory, which also fixes the reading it would otherwise have taken beside prefs.js.
2026-09-12T11:27Z claude note TWO RULINGS OF 2026-09-12 REACH THE RIG, and one of them reopens a defect this ticket had just closed. (a) 0772's ruling -- an uninstall writes enabled=false, so a reinstall starts in Off, no modal, button and window installed -- is right for a user and a trap for the bench: the randomised arm draws uninstall-then-install about one cycle in ten, and once 0773 lands every such draw would leave the sitter Off for the REST of the run, with no error, no prompt and nothing in the log. That is 0778's defect exactly, reopened by an unrelated and correct decision. The rig now re-answers the pref after an uninstall and, on every action that is not a plain replace, READS liveness again rather than assuming the write took; a sitter that stops being alive at cycle 7 ends the run, because every cycle after it is uninterpretable in the way arm 5 was uninterpretable throughout. (b) 0777's plafond stands at 10.* and 0776 is still open, and between them they will make the END OF LIFE of this plugin on a host 11 look exactly like the defect: out of range and absent from its update manifest is the configuration update.json's own comment accuses of the disable-then-delete this ticket is about. So the watcher's transition line now carries the host version (read from compatibility.ini, not over RDP, so it survives Zotero being down) and the recorded strict_max_version. A Zotero UPGRADE is thereby its own logged line, stamped, sitting immediately above whatever follows it -- without which a disappearance read six months from now could not be told from an ordinary end of life. Two arms each, the unreadable case paired with the readable one. Both changes are in bench/ only: nothing here touches the plugin, the manifest, the mutants or the release notes, which are the four zones PR 531 is finalising.
2026-09-12T12:54Z claude note MERGED ONTO 0771 AND THE FOUR SIBLING LANES, and the merge exposes a gap neither ticket owns alone. Resolution as 0771's own plan specified: nameBootstrapReason(reason) replaces the hoisted expression, 0771's handle-removal block stays where it is, and writeSDTDeathCertificate(named) runs AFTER it -- unharmed, because the certificate reads the local `journal` binding and not the global that block may have just deleted. Version 0.3.47 (0771 took 0.3.46), and my seven certificate mutants renumbered M47-M53: 0771 had taken M40-M46 for the lifecycle interface. 53/53 caught, 63 scenarios, 1374 pytest, erg check PASS over 336 tickets. THE GAP: 0771 removes the parked ring on uninstall because an uninstall has no next activation to read it; this ticket parks it because the reader is OUT OF BAND -- a human in Run JavaScript, or bench/sitter_watch.py over RDP, both reading a process that is still alive. Both readings are true, and taking 0771's half alone has a consequence that is now stated in bootstrap.js at the site and in RELEASE-NOTES.md where an installer meets it: WITH THE DEBUG SWITCH OFF, WHICH IS HOW A RELEASE SHIPS, AN UNINSTALL NOW LEAVES NOTHING -- no certificate, because the switch gates it, and no parked ring, because 0771 deleted it. Before 0771 the ring at least outlived the removal for the rest of the session. Not closed here, because closing it means either keeping the ring on uninstall or writing the certificate unconditionally on that one reason, and both are the author's to rule on. The release note tells the reader to switch diagnostics on before reporting a disappearance, which is the mitigation available without a ruling.
2026-09-12T14:27Z claude note THE OPEN ARBITRAGE IS CLOSED, against this ticket and correctly. Asked whether the parked ring should survive an uninstall, the author ruled on 2026-09-12: in a live install a CLEAN UNINSTALL is the way (DECISIONS.md). So 0771's handle removal is the behaviour and not a compromise, and the consequence this ticket flagged at the merge is ACCEPTED rather than owed: with the diagnostics switch off, which is how a release ships, an uninstall leaves nothing -- no certificate, because the switch gates it, and no parked ring, because 0771 deleted it. Neither way of closing it is taken. The reasoning I had not weighed heavily enough: this ticket's evidence is not worth a handle left behind on every machine that ever uninstalled this add-on, and a plugin that has been removed should not still be reachable from the host it was removed from. What remains, and it is enough: the evidence path for an uninstall is deliberately OPT-IN -- the operator turns diagnostics on before reporting a disappearance, which RELEASE-NOTES.md asks of a reader, and the certificate is then the only durable record there will ever be. Ticket 0773 now carries the exception that a clean uninstall must not sweep that one file away. bootstrap.js's comment at the site is rewritten from "the author's to rule on" to the ruling. 0.3.48.
--- body ---
## Context

The author has reported for weeks that the sitter "tends to disappear on its
own from the installed-plugins list". Ticket 0688 investigated, ruled out both
mechanisms it had named (no Zotero update happened; the install used Tools ->
Add-ons -> Install Add-on From File, the persistent path), declined to invent
a third, and closed with the cause open and instrumentation added so the next
occurrence would leave evidence.

It occurred twice on the evening of 2026-09-06, during live verification, and
this time the evidence was collected while it was happening.

## Read the log before this body (corrected 2026-09-11)

Everything below is the ticket **as filed on 2026-09-06**. The log has
superseded it on three points, and a reader who takes the body at face value
will re-run refuted work. This section exists because that just happened.

1. **The mechanism in the title and in "The mechanism this points at" is
   REFUTED.** 0.2.15 shipped with `update.json` advertising a *compatible*
   version and died on the same schedule as the empty-list build, disabled at
   +20 s and deleted at +40 s. The timing never fitted either:
   `extensions.update.interval` is 86400, a daily check, against a disable
   twenty seconds after install. The title is kept only so the id stays
   greppable (log, 2026-09-06T22:34Z).
2. **Action 1 below cannot be run at all.** It asks for a build with
   `update_url` REMOVED. Settled with a control the same evening: `update_url`
   is REQUIRED — the identical build without it (0.2.14) is refused at install
   with "peut-etre incompatible avec cette version de Zotero". This also
   corrects 0688's docs-based reading that only `applications.zotero` need be
   present. Actions 2 and 3, which branch off Action 1's outcome, fall with it.
3. **Four deliberate arms have since been run and all survived** — first
   install, single replacement, pre-fix payload, disable/re-enable, across two
   machines. The ticket was closed as parked on 2026-09-08 and **reopened the
   same evening on a live recurrence**: five consecutive GUI replacements,
   0.3.15 through 0.3.19, into one process never restarted, over roughly an
   hour. Arm 2 tested exactly ONE replacement.

**The live candidate is therefore cumulative rapid replacement in a single long
session, and no bounded experiment has excluded plain intermittency.** That arm
needs many unattended cycles, which is what Action 4's instrument was built
for. Do not re-run arms 1 to 3: they were run deliberately, with controls, and
they answered.

## What was observed

One Zotero session, started 20:56:31, never restarted:

- 23:0x  `verify` reports `present, version 0.2.13, active False, location app-profile`
- 23:35:59  `extensions/` and `extensions.json` both mtime-stamped; the record
  is gone and `extensions/sdt-pack-sitter@....xpi` is deleted from disk
- The running instance kept working from memory throughout -- the author could
  still open and close the dialog, and the toolbar spinner still animated.

So: registered, then DISABLED, then REMOVED, file and all, mid-session, with no
Zotero update and no user action. Disable-then-remove is what Gecko's
AddonManager does to an add-on whose update/compatibility check concludes there
is no compatible version of it.

## The mechanism this points at

`manifest.json` carries `applications.zotero.update_url`, and Zotero fetches it
on a timer after startup -- which is the one thing in this plugin that happens
minutes into a session, unprompted, matching the observed timing.

That URL resolves (HTTP 200) and returns a well-formed Mozilla update manifest
naming this add-on with an EMPTY updates array:

    "addons": { "sdt-pack-sitter@search-works-for-zotero.invalid": { "updates": [] } }

The reading to test is that Zotero takes "your id is listed, and it has no
versions" as "there is no compatible version of you", disables, and cleans up.

**This is why 0688's fix did not stop it.** 0688 changed `update_url` from
`https://example.invalid/...` (unreachable -> no version information) to a
reachable manifest carrying an empty list (-> still no version information).
Both hand Zotero the same answer. The change was a real fix to a real defect --
a URL chosen to never resolve -- and it did not touch this mechanism, which
nobody had identified. `update.json`'s own comment says as much and says it
honestly: "No claim is made either way about what an empty list or an
unreachable update_url does to an installed add-on -- that was not established
against Zotero's own fork."

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/manifest.json` - carries `applications.zotero.update_url`
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/update.json` - the empty-updates manifest it names
- `bench/sdt_sitter_install.py` - `verify` reads the host's own record, and is
  what distinguishes the three states this defect passes through
- `tickets/closed/0688-sitter-persistent-install-and-manifest-id.erg` - the
  investigation this continues; read its log before adding a fourth guess

## Actions

**Actions 1 to 3 are VOID as written — see "Read the log before this body".
`update_url` cannot be removed (the build is refused at install), and the
mechanism they isolate is refuted. They are left in place unstruck because the
reasoning that produced them is still worth reading, and because deleting a
refuted arm is how it gets proposed again.**

1. Isolate, one variable. Build the current payload with `update_url` REMOVED
   and nothing else changed, install it, and leave Zotero running past the
   window in which the removal has been seen (~40 min). Zotero's own developer
   docs list only `applications.zotero` itself as required; 0688 read them and
   recorded that `update_url` "is nowhere stated as required". 0688 deliberately
   kept it rather than change two variables at once on a plugin already
   disappearing -- correct then, and the live reproduction is what now justifies
   testing the other arm.
   Probe build: 0.2.14, `update_url` removed, identical otherwise.
2. If it survives, that is the mechanism. Decide the permanent shape: drop
   `update_url` (no update channel exists, so it advertises nothing anyone can
   install), or publish a real entry per release with `update_link` and version
   bounds. Do not leave an empty list.
3. If it does NOT survive, the mechanism is elsewhere and this ticket has
   narrowed it by one: record that, and do not close the ticket with a guess.
   The next candidate to examine is the `.invalid` id under a host that may
   validate ids it never dereferences.
4. ~~NEW, blocking 1-3 as filed: get a build installed at all, without GUI
   automation.~~ **DISCHARGED 2026-09-11.** Candidate (a) was built and merged:
   `bench/zotero_rdp_client.py` with `bench/zotero_arm_devtools_server.js`,
   ticket 0766, PR #523, against `--start-debugger-server`'s Remote Debugging
   Protocol. Candidate (b), asking the author to install by hand each cycle, is
   no longer needed and should not be asked for.

   Two defects were found in that client by its own review before it merged,
   and both are of this ticket's favourite family, the silent failure: replies
   were matched by actor name alone, so a late reply from a timed-out earlier
   call could be attributed to the next one (`d67ed60` matches by shape and
   ends the connection on timeout), and `eval_js` did not unwrap a promise
   result, swallowing a rejection (`fa9e89e`). Both are fixed on main. They are
   recorded here because they set the condition under which this client may be
   used as an instrument, in the Test section below.

## Test

**The instrument this section names is gone (corrected 2026-09-11).**
`scratchpad/sitter-watch.sh` ran — the log's 2026-09-06T22:34Z note reads six
cycles off `scratchpad/sitter-watch.log`, and it is what carried arms 1 to 3.
But `git log --all --diff-filter=A -- "*sitter-watch*"` finds it added on no
branch ever, and `scratchpad/` is not in the tree today. The evidence it
produced survives only as prose quoted in this log. Nothing is watching now, so
no arm can produce a verdict until it is rebuilt, whoever or whatever performs
the install.

This is the blocker for the remaining arm. The install is not: Action 4's
instrument exists and is merged.

Rebuild it, and commit it this time. A file a later workpackage consumes is
durable state, not scratch (`rules/git.md`, don't gitignore handoff artifacts).
What it must do is unchanged: poll the three facts 0688's three-state model
separates -- record present, record active, XPI on disk -- plus Zotero's pid,
and append a line ONLY on a change, so a quiet log reads "nothing moved" and a
line is a stamped transition.

Two conditions this ticket did not previously state:

- **A positive control before any quiet log is believed.** Provoke a transition
  deliberately -- disable the add-on by hand, or move the XPI -- and watch the
  watcher emit a line. A silent log and a blind watcher are the same artifact
  until one of them has been seen to fire.
- **The observation channel stays out of band from the RDP client.** The client
  may perform the install; it must not be what reports the resulting state.
  Both defects found in it before merge were silent failures, so a reading
  taken through it cannot distinguish a true state from a misattributed reply.
  `bench/sdt_sitter_install.py verify` reads the host's own record, and the
  manifest inside the installed `.xpi` on disk says whether `update_url` is
  really absent. Confirm the payload that way before starting the clock.

## Verification

- [ ] The probe build (no `update_url`) survives a Zotero session longer than
      any build carrying one has
- [ ] `sitter-watch.log` shows the survival as an absence of transitions, over
      a window that previously contained one
- [ ] A build WITH `update_url` restored disappears again -- the control that
      makes the survival mean something rather than a quiet evening

## Invariants

- Do not "fix" this by having the installer reinstall the plugin on a timer.
  That hides a host decision the plugin should not be arguing with, and would
  make the next occurrence invisible again.
- `bench/check_sitter_version.py`'s guard stands: any payload change needs its
  own version number, probe builds included.
- The verdict never travels through the instrument that performs the install.
  Action 1 is decided by out-of-band observation of the host's own state, which
  is why it does not inherit the RDP client's failure modes and why it is the
  cheap arm. The volume arm is the opposite case: many cycles, timeouts, and
  exactly the conditions under which a reply-matching defect would manufacture
  or erase the intermittency it is meant to measure.

## Exit criteria

The disappearance has a named, demonstrated mechanism and a fix whose control
arm has been run in both directions -- or, if `update_url` is exonerated, this
ticket records that with its evidence and hands on the next candidate.
